## Why this fork exists

EDS Lite's source has not been touched since 2020-04-21. Its successor, EDS NG,
is closed source and paywalls the features that matter most for the threat model
this fork targets: hidden volumes, keyfiles, and container-wide search.

Those features are not all in the same state, and the difference decides how much work
each one is. Measured by enumerating every reference in app/src, not inferred from the
method names:

HIDDEN VOLUMES are present and switched off. The container layer carries the plumbing
throughout: EdsContainerBase.tryLayout(..., isHidden) at line 226 branches on it,
line 253 refuses the hidden pass with `if(isHidden && !cf.hasHiddenContainerSupport())`,
getLayouts(boolean isHidden) selects the layout, and truecrypt/StdLayout reserves four
header slots (RESERVED_HEADER_SIZE = 4*HEADER_SIZE) of which two are the hidden header
and its backup. StdLayout.decodeHeader already reads the data-area start from header
offset 108 and the volume size from 116, so a layout pointed at the hidden header opens
the hidden volume with no change to the crypto path. What the lite build did was answer
"no" in two methods of app/src/lite/java/com/sovworks/eds/truecrypt/FormatInfo.java,
hasHiddenContainerSupport() and getHiddenVolumeLayout(). FDS answers yes and supplies
truecrypt/HiddenLayout and veracrypt/HiddenVolumeLayout, each of which overrides exactly
two offset methods. Opening only: creating a hidden volume needs the outer volume's free
space and outer-volume write protection. (FDS has since added outer-volume protection;
creation is still not implemented.)

KEYFILES are absent, not disabled, and saying otherwise would overstate what this fork
inherits. The whole tree contains three references to the feature and none of them is an
implementation: the interface declaration in container/ContainerFormatInfo.java, the lite
override, and luks/FormatInfoBase.java. There is no keyfile pool, no CRC32 mixer, no UI.
hasKeyfilesSupport() -> false is an honest answer here. FDS has to write the feature.
(Since then FDS has written the keyfile pool, truecrypt/Keyfiles.java, tested it against
desktop VeraCrypt, and added a keyfile picker to the unlock dialog. hasKeyfilesSupport() is
now true for TrueCrypt and VeraCrypt, for opening only.)

MAXIMUM PASSPHRASE LENGTH was a plain constant, getMaxPasswordLength() -> 64, inherited by
veracrypt/FormatInfo without an override. TrueCrypt's format limit really is 64 bytes, so
that answer was right for TrueCrypt and wrong for VeraCrypt, whose limit is 128.
EdsContainerBase.cutPassword truncates silently, and a truncated passphrase derives a
different key, so a 90-character VeraCrypt passphrase failed to open its own container with
nothing to say why. veracrypt/FormatInfo now answers 128.

FDS keeps the engine, removes the paywall, and writes what was never there.
